    "faq": {
      "type": "bella-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does this meet California's disclosure requirements?",
            "answer": "Yes. California's AB 723 requires a statement on or next to any digitally altered listing image, plus access to the original, unaltered photo — posted alongside it or through a link. San Diego orders ship with disclosure wording; keep your original photos to post or link next to the staged set. Your broker or MLS has the final say on exactly how it appears."
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you stage an ADU or converted garage?",
            "answer": "Yes, and it's worth doing. An empty ADU photographs as storage; staged as a small studio it reads as rental income or an in-law suite, which is usually why the buyer is interested in the property at all."
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "A lot of my buyers are military, relocating on short notice. Does that matter?",
            "answer": "It puts pressure on turnaround more than anything. A PCS-ing buyer house-hunts inside a narrow window, so photos coming back in 24 to 48 hours means your listing is live while they're actually looking."
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you stage outdoor space?",
            "answer": "Yes. In coastal San Diego the patio, deck or indoor-outdoor room is often the strongest thing about the property, and it's the room that suffers most from being photographed empty."
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much do stagers charge to stage a house in San Diego?",
            "answer": "Physical stagers quote by the size of the home, the number of rooms furnished and how long the furniture stays, so San Diego quotes vary widely. As a general benchmark, conventional staging runs about $2,000–5,000 a month until the home sells. Virtual staging from Bella is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, paid once, with no rental period to extend if the listing sits."
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much do people charge for virtual staging?",
            "answer": "Prices vary by provider and by whether a person or an AI app does the work. Bella's designer-made virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} per photo, with volume discounts of 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+. Furniture removal for occupied rooms, unlimited revisions for two weeks and full usage rights are included — no subscription, no minimum."
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is staging a house really worth it? Does staging sell a house?",
            "answer": "For most vacant or dated listings, yes. Staging can't guarantee a sale, but 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, according to the National Association of Realtors, so the photos decide which homes get a showing. NAR's research on home staging also finds that staging makes it easier for buyers to visualize a property as their future home. Virtual staging gives those photos a furnished, market-ready look for a one-time cost per photo instead of monthly furniture rental."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging legal in California?",
            "answer": "Yes, as long as it isn't misleading. Under AB 723 (California Business and Professions Code §10140.8), a digitally altered listing image needs a conspicuous statement on or next to it saying it was altered, plus access to the original, unaltered image — posted alongside it or through a link or QR code. Add furniture and decor only; don't hide defects or change permanent features. Every Bella order includes disclosure guidance, and your broker or MLS has the final say on placement."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the best virtual staging program?",
            "answer": "It depends on the listing. DIY programs and AI apps are fast and cheap, but they can distort scale, finishes and light. A virtual staging company like Bella has a real interior designer compose each room, which keeps furniture true to the room's dimensions — important when a buyer walks in and compares."
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you show a room in more than one style, or a home that isn't built yet?",
            "answer": "Yes. Because the furniture is digital, a room can be staged in more than one Look Book style, which makes it easy to test which look suits the buyer. For dated rooms, virtual renovation (from {price:virtual-renovation-service}) shows new floors, walls and finishes; for homes that aren't built yet, 3D rendering (from {price:3d-rendering}) creates photorealistic images from plans."
          }
        },
        "q11": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How can I virtually stage my home for free?",
            "answer": "Free AI apps and DIY software can drop furniture into a photo, but the results often distort scale and finishes, and a misleading image creates disclosure problems. New clients can claim a $40 voucher on Bella's sign-up page to try designer-made staging, subject to Bella's approval."
          }
        },
        "q12": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is home staging in demand in San Diego?",
            "answer": "Staging remains a standard part of listing prep, and it matters most where buyers shop remotely — which describes much of San Diego's military and relocation market. Virtual staging meets the same need for the online listing without furniture rental, and it works for vacant, occupied and ADU photos alike."
          }
        }
      },
